<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-s07e02" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Episode 2: Solving Multi-step Equations</title>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Episode 2 Learning Goals</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            	Solve equations that have variables on both sides of the = sign.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <p>
    When we’re solving an equation that has variables on both sides of the = sign, we start by “moving” all the terms that contain a variable to one side of the = sign. 
    Then, we move all the terms that don’t contain a variable to the other side of the = sign.
  </p>
  <p>
    <alert>To Solve Equations that Have Variables on Both Sides</alert>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          Add or subtract terms to cancel out all the variables on one side of the equation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Clean up the equation by combining like terms.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Add or subtract terms to cancel out all the numbers on the other side of the equation.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Clean up the equation by combining like terms.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          If necessary, finish isolating the variable using multiplication or division.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve the equation:
      <md>
        3x+1 = 2x+4
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Solution 1:</alert> Let’s try moving all the x terms to the left side of the =.
    </p>
    <p>
      To get rid of the <m>2x</m> on the right side, we can subtract <m>2x</m>. 
      Since we’re subtracting <m>2x</m> on the right side, we must also subtract <m>2x</m> on the left to keep the equation balanced:
      <md>
        3x+1-2x=2x+4-2x
      </md>
      Now let’s clean this up by combining like terms:
      <md>
        x+1=4
      </md>
      Now let’s move all the numbers to the right side of the =. To get rid of the 1 on the left, we can subtract 1 from both sides:
      <md>
        x+1-1=4-1
      </md>
      <md>
        x=3
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Solution 2:</alert> Let’s try moving all the x terms to the right side of the =.
    </p>
    <p>
      To get rid of the <m>3x</m> on the left, we can subtract <m>3x</m> from both sides:
      <md>
        3x+1-3x=2x+4-3x
      </md>
      Now we combine like terms:
      <md>
        1=4-x
      </md>
      Now let’s move all the numbers to the left side. To get rid of the 4 on the right, we subtract 4 from both sides.
      <md>
        1-4=4-x-4
      </md>
      <md>
        -3=-x
      </md>
      Now, to get rid of the multiplied (-1) on the <m>x</m>, we divide by  (-1):
    
    <md>
      \frac{-3}{-1} = \frac{-x}{-1}
    </md>
    <md>
    3=x
    </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <assemblage>
    <title>Notice Something Neat!</title>
    <p>
      It doesn’t matter whether we move the variables to the left side or to the right side of the equation. You get the same answer in the end either way.
    </p>
  </assemblage>
  <example>
    <p>
      Solve:
      <md>
        \frac{1}{4}x+4=\frac{1}{3}x
      </md>
    </p>
    <p>
      Solution: Let’s try moving all the <m>x</m> terms to the right side of the equation. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Tp get rid of the <m>\frac{1}{4}x</m> on the left, we can subtract <m>\frac{1}{4}x</m> from both sides:
      <md>
        \frac{1}{4}x + 4 -\frac{1}{4}x = \frac{1}{3}x-\frac{1}{4}x
      </md>
      <md>
        4=\frac{1}{3}x-\frac{1}{4}x
      </md>
      Now we need to combine the like terms, which means we need to combine the coefficients:
      <md>
        \frac{1}{3}-\frac{1}{4}
      </md>
      <md>
        \frac{1\times 4}{3\times 4} - \frac{1\times 2}{4\times 3}
      </md>
      <md>
        \frac{4}{12}-\frac{3}{12}
      </md>
      <md>
        \frac{1}{12}
      </md>
      This means our equation becomes:
      <md>
        4=\frac{1}{12}x
      </md>
      Finally, we divide both sides by 1/12 to get rid of the multiplied 1/12:
      <md>
        4\div \frac{1}{12} = \frac{1}{12}x\div \frac{1}{12}
      </md>
      <md>
        4\div \frac{1}{12} = x
      </md>
      <md>
        4\times 12 = x
      </md>
      <md>
        48=x
      </md>
    </p>
  </example>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-s07e02">
    <title>Season 7, Episode 2 Practice Problems</title>
    <p>
      Solve the following equations:
    </p>
    <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> 3x+5=7x-9 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> -x-7=4x+3</m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> -2x+4=x+10 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> 2.5x+1.5=-0.5x+6 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> 0.5x-3=2x+1 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> \frac{1}{2}x-5=\frac{1}{3}x+1 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> \frac{1}{2}x+6=\frac{3}{4}x-2 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> 9x+2=-16 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> 8x=3x+15 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> 7=2x-5</m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> -6x+12=-2x-4 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> 4x-6=0.25x+3</m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> \frac{5}{2}x=\frac{1}{2}x+8 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> -3x=5x+8</m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> 1.2x-4=3x+2</m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> 6x+1.5=2x-2.5 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> 7x+9=-x+1 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> \frac{7}{4}x-3=\frac{1}{4}x+5 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> \frac{3}{5}x+10 = \frac{8}{5}x-5 </m></p>
    </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="1.0in">
      <p><m> 10x-20=2x </m></p>
    </exercise>
  </worksheet>
</section> 